coder › Done. I implemented `toRoman` and `fromRoman` in `src/roman.ts` on a new branch `implement-roman`, and added tests in `src/roman.test.ts`.

What changed
- `toRoman(n)` builds the numeral from the standard digit tables (including the subtractive forms `IV`, `IX`, `XL`, `XC`, `CD`, `CM`) and throws a `RangeError` for anything that isn't an integer in `1..3999` — this covers `0`, negatives, `4000`, `1.5`, `NaN` and infinities via `Number.isInteger`.
- `fromRoman(text)` upper-cases the input so either case is accepted, validates it against a canonical-form regex (`^M{0,3}(?:CM|CD|D?C{0,3})(?:XC|XL|L?X{0,3})(?:IX|IV|V?I{0,3})$`), and throws a `SyntaxError` naming the original text for anything else (empty string, `IIII`, `VX`, `IC`, whitespace, etc.). Valid numerals are then evaluated left-to-right, subtracting when a symbol precedes a larger one.
- Tests cover named examples, case-insensitivity, the invalid forms, error types/messages, and a full round-trip `fromRoman(toRoman(n)) === n` for every `n` in `1..3999`.

